mode: "100644"
text: "# Samples Guide — sourcing the right writing\n\nThe analysis is only as good as the samples. Garbage in, generic out. This guide gets you\nmaterial that actually reveals the voice.\n\n## How many\n\n- **Ideal:** 5–10 substantial samples.\n- **Floor:** 3. Below that, proceed but mark the voice guide low-confidence and name the gaps.\n- **Ghostwriting at scale:** lean toward 10+, spanning the formats you'll write for.\n\nMore isn't always better — three samples that are unmistakably *them* beat fifteen that are\ninconsistent or committee-edited.\n\n## What makes a good sample\n\n- **Actually written by the person.** Not ghostwritten by someone else, not legal/marketing\n  boilerplate, not AI-generated. You're capturing *their* voice.\n- **Something they're proud of / feels like them.** Ask: \"Show me 3 things you've written that\n  felt the most *you*.\" Their judgment is a useful signal.\n- **In a natural register.** Off-the-cuff often beats polished — less self-editing, more voice.\n- **Relevant format.** If you'll write LinkedIn posts, weight LinkedIn samples. But mix in\n  other formats too; the through-line across formats *is* the voice.\n\n## The best source most people forget: spoken transcripts\n\nTranscripts of the person **talking** — a podcast, a webinar, a voice memo, a Loom, a sales\ncall — are gold. People's spoken voice is their most natural; writing tends to formalize it.\nA 5-minute voice memo answering \"why did you start this?\" often reveals more voice than ten\npolished posts. Ask for one.\n\nNote the caveat: spoken language has filler (\"um,\" \"like,\" false starts) and run-ons. Capture\nthe *rhythm and word choice*; record a cleanup rule for the written version (e.g., \"keep the\nconversational openers, cut the filler, break run-ons into short sentences\").\n\n## Sample types, ranked roughly by signal\n\n1. Spoken transcripts (most natural voice).\n2. Personal posts / threads they wrote themselves.\n3. Replies, comments, DMs (unguarded, conversational).\n4. Newsletters / blog posts (more considered, still them).\n5. Marketing/website copy (often committee voice — use cautiously).\n\n## What to exclude\n\n- Anything ghostwritten or heavily edited by others.\n- Legal, compliance, or boilerplate copy.\n- AI-generated drafts.\n- Clear outliers (a one-off formal piece that isn't representative).\n- Old material if the voice has since evolved (ask what's current).\n\n## When samples conflict (voice drift)\n\nIf samples disagree — formal in one place, loose in another — don't average them. Surface it:\n\"Your newsletter sounds buttoned-up; your X posts are loose and funny. Which is the voice you\nwant everywhere — or do they stay different by channel?\" Then anchor on the target, and capture\nthe channel split as tone variation, not a contradiction.\n\n## Aspirational voice\n\nIf the person wants to sound *different* than they currently do, collect a second set:\n**samples of writers/brands they want to sound like**. Label them clearly as aspirational. In\nthe analysis, separate \"your current voice\" from \"your target voice,\" and be transparent about\nwhat's being borrowed so the result still feels honest and ownable — not an impersonation.\n\n## Quick intake script\n\nIf the user doesn't know what to share, ask for:\n1. \"The 3 things you've written that felt the most *you*.\"\n2. \"A voice memo or transcript of you explaining what you do, like you'd tell a friend.\"\n3. \"A reply or DM where you were just being yourself.\"\n\nThat's usually enough to start.\n"
